(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-10"] = {"16.1":{"s":"Food supplies nutrients that give energy and help the body grow and repair itself. The nutrients are carbohydrates, lipids (fats), proteins, vitamins, minerals and water. Food quality is judged by flavour, smell, texture, colour and spoilage by microbes. Oxygen, enzymes and microbes spoil stored food, so it is kept airtight or cold, and some packed foods contain antioxidants as additives to last longer.","w":["Keep apart the roles: carbohydrates and fats give energy, proteins build and repair the body.","A food additive has to be safe within the permitted amount; artificial sweeteners are not a source of energy in the same way as sugar."],"x":["Food supplies the nutrients that give us energy, help us grow, and let the body repair itself. The main nutrients are carbohydrates, lipids (fats and oils), proteins, vitamins, minerals and water. Carbohydrates and fats are the main sources of energy: 1 g of fat gives about 37 kJ, and 1 g of carbohydrate or protein about 17 kJ. Proteins are needed mainly to build and repair the tissues. Vitamins and minerals are needed in small amounts, to keep the body working well.","The quality of food is judged by its flavour, smell, texture and colour, and by whether it has spoiled. Food spoils because oxygen, enzymes and microbes act on it. To slow the spoilage, food is kept airtight, cold, dry, or salted or sweetened, or it is treated with preservatives such as sodium benzoate. Antioxidants, such as BHA and BHT, are added to oils and snacks, since they react with oxygen first and stop the fat from going rancid.","Food additives include preservatives, antioxidants, colours, flavours and sweeteners. Artificial sweeteners, such as saccharin and aspartame, are many times sweeter than sugar, and give almost no energy, so they are used by diabetics. They are safe only within the permitted amount. Aspartame is not used for cooking, since it breaks down when it is heated.","Common mistake: thinking that all the nutrients give energy. Vitamins, minerals and water do not give energy."],"e":"A food has 20 g of carbohydrate, 10 g of fat and 5 g of protein. Its energy is 20 × 17 + 10 × 37 + 5 × 17 = 795 kJ, which is about 190 kcal. Fat gives nearly half of the total, though it has only 10 g."},"16.2":{"s":"Some chemical compounds act as medicines. Analgesics reduce pain, and antipyretics bring down fever; paracetamol and aspirin are common examples. Antimicrobials kill or stop the growth of microbes, and include antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants. Antiseptics are mild enough to apply to living tissue, but disinfectants are used on objects and are too strong for skin. A drug must be taken only in the right dose.","w":["Antiseptic is used on the body (such as dilute iodine); disinfectant on floors and instruments (such as phenol in strong solution).","Antibiotics act on bacteria, not on viruses."],"x":["Chemicals that are used to treat diseases or to relieve their symptoms are called drugs or medicines. A drug must be taken in the right dose, since too much can be harmful, and too little may not work.","Analgesics reduce pain without causing unconsciousness. Aspirin and paracetamol are non-narcotic analgesics. Antipyretics reduce fever. Paracetamol is both an analgesic and an antipyretic, and aspirin also reduces inflammation and thins the blood. Antacids, such as sodium bicarbonate and magnesium hydroxide, reduce the acidity in the stomach.","Antimicrobials kill microbes or stop their growth. They include antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants. Antibiotics, such as penicillin, are made by microorganisms, or in the laboratory, and they act on bacteria. They do not work against viruses. Taking them without need makes bacteria resistant. An antiseptic is mild enough to use on living tissue, such as a cut, as with dilute iodine, boric acid and 0.2 percent phenol. A disinfectant is stronger, and is used on objects such as floors, toilets and instruments, as with 1 percent phenol and bleaching powder, and it harms the skin.","Common mistake: using an antibiotic for a viral infection, such as a cold or flu. It does not help."],"e":"To make an antiseptic from a stock of 5 percent phenol, dilute 20 mL of the stock to 500 mL. The result is 0.2 percent phenol. A 1 percent solution of the same chemical is a disinfectant, and not for the skin."},"16.3":{"s":"Cleansing agents remove dirt, which is mostly oily and sticks to the surface. Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids, and detergents are synthetic compounds with a similar structure. A molecule has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail. In water the tails surround the oily dirt and form tiny balls called micelles, which are washed away. Soaps do not work well in hard water, but detergents do.","f":[{"x":"CH₃(CH₂)₁₁OSO₃⁻ Na⁺","n":"Sodium lauryl sulphate. A common synthetic detergent (anionic)."},{"x":"2C₁₇H₃₅COONa + Ca²⁺ → (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂Ca ↓ + 2Na⁺","n":"Soap in hard water. Insoluble scum forms; detergents do not do this."}],"w":["Soap forms a scum with the calcium and magnesium ions of hard water; most detergents do not.","Detergents with branched chains are not broken down by bacteria and cause water pollution; straight-chain ones are biodegradable."],"x":["Cleansing agents remove dirt from skin, cloth and dishes. Most dirt is oily and sticks to the surface, so water alone cannot remove it, since oil does not mix with water. Soaps are the sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids, such as sodium stearate, C₁₇H₃₅COONa. They are made by heating a fat or an oil with NaOH (saponification), with glycerol as a by-product. The soap is separated by adding salt.","A soap or detergent molecule has a water-loving (hydrophilic) head, the –COO⁻ or –SO₃⁻ group, and an oil-loving (hydrophobic) tail, the long hydrocarbon chain. In water, the tails go into the drop of oil, and the heads stay in the water. This forms tiny balls called micelles, which carry the oil, and they repel each other, so they do not join. The water washes them away.","Soaps do not work well in hard water, which has Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions. They form an insoluble scum of calcium and magnesium salts: 2C₁₇H₃₅COONa + Ca²⁺ → (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂Ca + 2Na⁺. This wastes soap. Synthetic detergents have the sulfate or sulfonate heads, such as sodium lauryl sulfate. Their calcium and magnesium salts are soluble, so they work in hard water and in acidic water.","Detergents with a branched chain are not broken down by the bacteria, and they cause foam and pollution in rivers. Detergents with a straight chain are biodegradable. Common mistake: saying that a detergent is a soap. A soap is a salt of a fatty acid, and a detergent is a synthetic compound."],"e":"Hard water has 0.20 g of calcium ions. The soap that is wasted in forming the scum is 0.20/40 × 2 × 306 = 3.06 g of sodium stearate. A detergent would not be wasted in this way."}};
